## HNI Standards

The standards library is served live by the `hni-standards` MCP server — not read from
a local checkout. This means grounding is never stale relative to what's actually merged
in `hni-ai-dev-standards`, unlike a locally cloned copy that can silently drift.

Available tools: `list_domains`, `list_standards`, `get_standard`, `search_standards`,
`get_applicable_standards`, `record_finding`, `record_waiver_request`,
`suggest_standard_change`, `list_mcp_servers`, `list_templates`, `get_template`. Call
`list_domains` any time to confirm the server is reachable.

**At the start of every session, call `list_domains()` first** — cheap (~27 domains, a
few hundred tokens), refreshing your map of what exists without paying for any
domain's actual content yet (fetch that lazily, only for domains a file/task actually
touches — don't front-load every domain's content, that's ~12.7K tokens wasted on any
session that doesn't end up touching most of the library). This rule being
`alwaysApply: true` means it's already reloaded at the start of every session, which is
what makes this instruction fire reliably without needing a separate hook. There's no
verified Cursor equivalent to Claude Code's post-compaction hook at the time this was
written — if Cursor summarizes or truncates earlier context during a long session,
self-detect that (you can't see something you're sure you fetched earlier, or a much
older tool result looks abbreviated) and treat it the same way: call `list_domains()`
again and re-fetch a standard's text before relying on it rather than trusting a
possibly-degraded memory of it.

If a standard seems wrong, outdated, or genuinely ambiguous for a case it should cover,
call `suggest_standard_change(standard_id, feedback, suggested_by)` rather than quietly
deciding it doesn't apply.

